{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1958","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1958","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1958\",\"parent_revision\":\"501b23dade1a018d7a5e8a2588296ae34587d3583b3b5148f4c443367588d8cb\",\"root_revision\":\"501b23dade1a018d7a5e8a2588296ae34587d3583b3b5148f4c443367588d8cb\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-05-02\\n-->\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Add \\\"expand acronyms\\\" naming convention rule to Python coding standards\\nDate: 2026-05-05\\nGoal: Add expand-acronyms identifier naming rule to python3-standards.md section 1.5 and propagate to python3-core/SKILL.md, the python-engineering duplicate standards file, and both code-reviewer SOP agent files.\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Primary standards file exists at expected path | AVAILABLE | ls confirmed; 210-line file present\\n2. Rule not already present in any target file | AVAILABLE | grep for \\\"Identifier Naming|acronym|expand acron|gcd()|abbreviat\\\" across all target files returned zero matches (verified 2026-05-05)\\n3. Section 1.5 unoccupied in primary standards file | AVAILABLE | heading grep confirms 1.4 (\\\"Performance\\\") at line 38 and 1.6 (\\\"Script Dependency Trade-offs\\\") at line 44; no 1.5 heading present\\n4. Duplicate python3-standards.md in python-engineering exists | AVAILABLE | ls confirmed plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md present (210 lines)\\n5. Duplicate file content baseline | AVAILABLE | diff shows exactly one difference: line 58 (Rich Width Handling bullet text); all other lines identical; section 1 headings identical in both files; section 1.5 insertion position is the same in both\\n6. Line-58 diff is content-only, not structural | AVAILABLE | diff output confirms only line 58 differs (within section 1.7 \\\"UI & CLI\\\"); section 1.5 insertion is unaffected\\n7. python3-core/SKILL.md \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" at known location | AVAILABLE | grep confirms \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" at line 11; no \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" section exists; insertion target confirmed\\n8. Both code-reviewer.md files exist | AVAILABLE | ls confirmed both paths present\\n9. Code-reviewer SOPs do not already enumerate \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" | AVAILABLE | grep returned zero matches in both agent files; current enumeration ends at \\\"Testing Standards\\\"\\n10. Source pattern text confirmed | AVAILABLE | TheAlgorithms-Python.md line 150 verbatim quote: \\\"Expand acronyms because `gcd()` is hard to understand but `greatest_common_divisor()` is not.\\\"\\n11. Target content fully specified | AVAILABLE | item Target State specifies all four elements: rule statement, contrast example pair, established-acronym exception list (URL, API, SQL, HTTP, JSON, XML), local-variable scope note (>5 lines)\\n12. markdownlint CI constraint known | AVAILABLE | MD031 required: code fences need `python` language specifier and blank lines before/after\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 12\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n\\nNEW FINDING \u2014 file drift at line 58:\\nThe two python3-standards.md copies are NOT byte-for-byte identical (the prior RT-ICA stated \\\"byte-for-byte identical\\\"). They diverge at line 58 on the Rich Width Handling bullet text (section 1.7). This divergence does NOT affect section 1.5 insertion. However, AC #5 (\\\"verified by diffing the two files' section 1.5 blocks\\\") must target section 1.5 specifically \u2014 a full-file diff will always show line-58 noise.\\n\\nImplementation order:\\n1. Edit plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md \u2014 insert section 1.5 \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" after the 1.4 block\\n2. Copy identical section 1.5 content to plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md (same insertion position confirmed)\\n3. Add naming rule reference inside \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" in plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md\\n4. Add \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" bullet to standards enumeration in plugins/python3-development/agents/code-reviewer.md (after \\\"Testing Standards\\\")\\n5. Add \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" bullet to standards enumeration in plugins/python-engineering/agents/code-reviewer.md (same position)\\n6. Run: uv run prek run --files <all five modified files> \u2014 verify exit 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` \u2014 primary target; new subsection 1.5 \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" is inserted here with the expand-acronyms rule, contrast example pair, established-acronym exception list, and local-variable scoping note | Risk: LOW | Why: additive content only; no existing rules are altered; section 1.5 is currently unoccupied\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md` \u2014 verbatim duplicate of the python3-development copy; confirmed identical content on lines 1-211; if this file is not updated in sync, two conflicting versions of the standards document will exist in the codebase simultaneously | Risk: HIGH | Why: the two files are byte-for-byte identical today \u2014 any update to one without the other creates a permanent divergence that agents loading either plugin will observe as contradictory standards\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\nNone identified. This change is to documentation/instruction content, not executable code.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/language-manifest.md` \u2014 declares the standards authority as `/python3-development:modernpython` and routes quality gates through `python3-standards.md` indirectly; adding an identifier naming rule may warrant a pointer or entry in the `standards` quality gate field so agents know the new section exists | Risk: LOW | Why: no structural change to the manifest is strictly required; the new section becomes accessible via the existing standards reference path\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\nNone identified. There are no automated tests for skill document content (rule text, examples, or section headings) in this repository. The `skilllint` hook validates frontmatter schema and file structure, not prose content correctness. No coverage gap for the naming rule itself is possible \u2014 the change is documentation only. However, the absence of content-drift detection between the two `python3-standards.md` copies means any future divergence will go undetected automatically.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` \u2014 line 9 says \\\"Consult `references/python3-standards.md` for the full standards document\\\"; the item proposes adding a \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" subsection here as a cross-reference or pointer; the Fact-Check (Claim 4) confirmed this section does not exist \u2014 it must be created inside the existing \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section; without this cross-reference, agents loading only the `python-engineering` plugin via `python3-core` receive the new rule only through the duplicate `python3-standards.md` copy | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: the item explicitly requires this cross-reference; if omitted, implementation is incomplete by the item's own acceptance criteria\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/SKILL.md` \u2014 lines 54-55 and 96-100 document `references/python3-standards.md` as the bundled reference for \\\"Architecture Standards, Python Standards, CLI Standards, Service Integration Standards, Testing Standards\\\"; the summary list at lines 96-100 currently enumerates five categories with no mention of identifier naming; once the new section is added, this enumeration becomes incomplete and could mislead agents into thinking naming is not covered | Risk: LOW | Why: the summary list is informational, not gatekeeping; agents reading the actual standards file will see the new rule; but the enumeration should be updated for accuracy\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 line 63 lists `skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md` as \\\"unified standards\\\"; this file will become outdated if the `python-engineering` copy is not updated | Risk: LOW | Why: the reference is valid; content staleness is the python3-standards duplicate problem, not a separate CLAUDE.md problem\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python-development-orchestration.md` \u2014 line 56 references `python3-development:code-reviewer` as the review agent that enforces the standards; the review agent's SOP explicitly loads `python3-standards.md` during review; if a new naming rule exists in the standards, reviewers using this agent will begin catching acronym violations in reviewed code; this is a behavioral consequence of the change, not a documentation gap, but implementers should know this is the downstream effect | Risk: LOW | Why: desired behavior change; no content update needed unless the orchestration guide lists what the reviewer checks\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` \u2014 the `skilllint` hook (line 144-151) runs `uvx skilllint@latest check --fix` on any committed `SKILL.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `agents/*.md` file; it validates frontmatter schema and structural correctness; the modified `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` and both `python3-standards.md` files will pass through this hook on commit; the new content is plain markdown prose and will not trigger schema violations | Risk: LOW | Why: skilllint validates structure, not content; new prose in a reference file and a new subsection in SKILL.md do not violate any frontmatter or schema constraints\\n\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` \u2014 the `markdownlint-cli2` hook (line 101-106) runs on all markdown files not excluded by pattern; both modified files are in `plugins/` and will be linted; the new section must use proper blank lines around fenced code blocks (MD031), consistent heading levels (MD001), and no trailing whitespace; contrast code examples require language specifiers on all code fences | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: inline code examples in the naming rule (```python gcd() vs greatest_common_divisor() example```) must be wrapped in a fenced block with `python` language specifier and blank lines before and after, otherwise markdownlint-cli2 will fail the pre-commit check and block the commit\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 lines 42-46 of this agent's SOP say \\\"Consult `../skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` when checking: Architecture Standards, Python Standards, CLI Standards, Service Integration Standards, Testing Standards\\\"; the enumerated categories do not include \\\"Identifier Naming\\\"; agents using this reviewer will load the new section because they load the whole file, but the explicit enumeration in the SOP could be updated to make the new category discoverable without reading the full document | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: the reviewer is the primary enforcement mechanism for these standards; making identifier naming explicit in its SOP checklist (alongside the other five categories) ensures it appears in review checklists and follow-up task descriptions \u2014 omitting it means naming violations may be caught inconsistently\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 loads `python-engineering:python3-core` which routes to the `python3-standards.md` duplicate; same SOP structure without explicit naming-standards enumeration; same risk as python3-development reviewer above | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: same enforcement-path gap as the python3-development reviewer\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/agents/python-cli-architect.md` \u2014 this agent produces Python code guided by the standards it loads; adding an identifier naming rule will affect what code it generates going forward (functions it writes should no longer use `gcd()`, `bfs()`, etc.); no explicit instruction update is needed since the agent reads `python3-standards.md` through its loaded skills, but the change represents a behavioral change in the agent's output | Risk: LOW | Why: behavioral change is the intended effect; agent loads standards automatically via skill dependencies; no instruction file update required\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/python-cli-architect.md` \u2014 same analysis as python3-development version above | Risk: LOW | Why: same reasoning; loads `python-engineering:python3-core` which loads `python3-standards.md`\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` | Role: producer | Connection: primary target file for the new section 1.5 | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md` | Role: producer | Connection: verbatim duplicate; must be updated in sync or divergence is created | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: line 9 references python3-standards.md as canonical standards; item requires adding Naming Defaults cross-reference here | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/python3-development/agents/code-reviewer.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: SOP line 42-46 explicitly enumerates categories to check from python3-standards.md; \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" not listed | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/code-reviewer.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: loads python3-core which routes to standards; same SOP enumeration gap | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/SKILL.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: lines 96-100 enumerate standards categories; new category not listed | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/CLAUDE.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: line 63 lists python3-standards.md as \\\"unified standards\\\"; content accuracy depends on duplicate sync | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python-development-orchestration.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: references code-reviewer as enforcement agent; naming violations will now be caught during review | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` | Role: ci | Connection: skilllint + markdownlint-cli2 hooks will run on modified SKILL.md and reference files; code fence formatting must be correct | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/python3-development/agents/python-cli-architect.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: produces Python code governed by standards; behavioral output changes when naming rule is active | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/python-cli-architect.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: same as python3-development version | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: MEDIUM\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md` \u2014 verbatim duplicate of the primary target; the two files are identical today; failing to update both in a single commit creates two conflicting Python standards documents in the same repository\\n  - `plugins/python3-development/agents/code-reviewer.md` and `plugins/python-engineering/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 both reviewers explicitly enumerate standard categories to check; \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" not in the list means the naming rule may be checked inconsistently and not surfaced in review checklists or follow-up task descriptions\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - documentation \u2014 duplicate standards file must stay in sync; two separate section-enumeration lists in SKILL.md and code-reviewer SOPs become stale\\n  - scope \u2014 the existence of a verbatim duplicate `python3-standards.md` in the `python-engineering` plugin was not described in the item; this doubles the required edit surface\\n  - operational \u2014 markdownlint-cli2 in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` will block commit if code fence examples omit language specifiers or blank lines\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - Verbatim duplicate `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/references/python3-standards.md` not mentioned in the item description; this file is the \\\"unified standards\\\" canonical source for the entire `python-engineering` plugin and all its agents; any implementation that modifies only the `python3-development` copy ships an incomplete change\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible \u2014 both `python3-standards.md` copies updated in the same commit\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible \u2014 N/A (no executable code consumers)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added \u2014 N/A (documentation change; no test coverage gap created)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated \u2014 `python3-core/SKILL.md` Naming Defaults cross-reference added; `python3-development/SKILL.md` Standards categories list updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated \u2014 markdownlint-cli2 and skilllint hooks verified to pass with properly formatted new content\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated \u2014 both `code-reviewer.md` agent SOPs updated to list \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" in the standards-check enumeration\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved \u2014 N/A (additive change, no deprecation)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked \u2014 `language-manifest.md` verified; `python-development-orchestration.md` verified; no further transitive consumers found\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add \\\"expand acronyms\\\" naming convention rule to python coding standards** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/learning-resources/TheAlgorithms-Python.md \u2014 pattern: Naming Conventions (\\\"Expand acronyms because gcd() is hard to understand\\\")\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-26\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:642cc62f1b1a1e6e245c2cf125b1c040 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-27)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md`\\n2. Search for section 1.5 \u2014 it is unoccupied (no content under that heading)\\n3. Search the entire file for the words \\\"acronym\\\", \\\"identifier\\\", or \\\"naming\\\" \u2014 no matches\\n4. Open `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md`, search \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" (lines 11-44) \u2014 no naming or acronym rule present\\n5. Observe: no file in either plugin enforces or even mentions expanding acronyms in identifiers\\n6. Produce a Python function named `gcd` \u2014 no agent guardrail fires; the name is accepted without warning\\n\\nGap confirmed: agents generating Python identifiers have zero guidance preventing opaque acronym names.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 P1\\n\\nRationale: The absence of this guardrail has an active, ongoing effect on code quality in every agent-generated Python artifact. Agents producing opaque acronym identifiers (gcd, bfs, lcm, dfs) create maintenance debt that compounds across sessions because no mechanism exists to catch the problem at generation time. The fix is bounded and low-risk (adding a rule to existing documents), and the source guidance already exists in the adjacent TheAlgorithms-Python.md reference. Deferring this leaves a known gap in the standards that agents rely on as authoritative.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Blocks**: Code reviewers lack a standard to cite when rejecting opaque identifier names; review feedback is subjective rather than rule-based\\n- **Affected files**: python3-standards.md, python3-core/SKILL.md, two code-reviewer.md SOP files, and a verbatim duplicate of python3-standards.md in the python-engineering plugin (all must be updated in sync)\\n- **Affected agents**: Every agent that consumes python3-standards.md or python3-core/SKILL.md as a coding standard \u2014 including all Python code generation and review agents\\n- **Bottleneck**: The gap in python3-standards.md section 1.5 is the root source; the verbatim duplicate and cross-reference files propagate the gap downstream\\n- **Severity**: Any Python function, class, or module name produced by an agent is unconstrained on acronym use; the problem recurs on every generation until the rule is present\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Agent-generated Python code will have consistently readable identifier names without requiring post-hoc review feedback\\n- Code reviewers gain a citable rule to reference when flagging opaque names, making reviews objective rather than stylistic\\n- The python3-standards.md section 1.5 placeholder becomes occupied, eliminating a visible gap in the standards document\\n- Established domain acronyms (URL, API, SQL, HTTP, JSON, XML) are explicitly preserved, preventing over-correction\\n- Local variable scope is also addressed, preventing short-lived opaque names from escaping into long-lived scope\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen the change is in place:\\n\\n- python3-standards.md section 1.5 contains an \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" subsection with an expand-acronyms rule, a contrast example (gcd vs greatest_common_divisor), a domain-acronym exception list, and a local variable scope rule\\n- python3-core/SKILL.md \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section references or restates the identifier naming rule\\n- All code-reviewer SOP checklists include \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" as a reviewable standard\\n- The verbatim duplicate of python3-standards.md in the python-engineering plugin contains the same section 1.5 content\\n- An agent generating a Python function for greatest common divisor will name it `greatest_common_divisor`, not `gcd`\\n- An agent generating a Python function that fetches from a URL will be permitted to retain `url` in the name because URL is on the established-acronym exception list\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `grep -n \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` returns at least one match in section 1.5\\n2. `grep -n \\\"greatest_common_divisor\\\" plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` returns the contrast example\\n3. `grep -n \\\"URL\\\\|API\\\\|SQL\\\\|HTTP\\\\|JSON\\\\|XML\\\" plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` returns the domain-acronym exception list within section 1.5\\n4. `grep -n \\\"Identifier Naming\\\\|acronym\\\" plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` returns at least one match in the \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section\\n5. The verbatim duplicate of python3-standards.md in the python-engineering plugin contains the same section 1.5 content \u2014 verified by diffing the two files' section 1.5 blocks\\n6. Both code-reviewer.md SOP files contain \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" in their standards checklist \u2014 verified by grep\\n7. `uv run prek run --files plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` exits 0 (markdownlint passes: code fences have language specifiers and blank lines)\\n8. `uv run prek run --files plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` exits 0\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Source reference | `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/TheAlgorithms-Python.md` line 150 \u2014 \\\"Expand acronyms because gcd() is hard to understand but greatest_common_divisor() is not.\\\" |\\n| Primary standards file | `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` \u2014 section 1.5 (currently unoccupied) |\\n| Cross-reference target | `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` \u2014 \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section (lines 11-44) |\\n| Skill | `/python3-development` \u2014 Python coding standards skill |\\n| Skill | `/python3-core` \u2014 Python core standards and defaults |\\n| CI linter | markdownlint-cli2 \u2014 requires code fence language specifiers and blank lines around fences |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Change needed |\\n|------|--------------|\\n| `plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` | Add \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" subsection to section 1.5 |\\n| `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` | Add naming rule reference or duplicate to \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section (lines 11-44) |\\n| Verbatim duplicate of python3-standards.md in python-engineering plugin | Must be updated in sync with the primary file (exact path to be confirmed during implementation) |\\n| First code-reviewer.md SOP file | Add \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" to standards checklist (exact path to be confirmed during implementation) |\\n| Second code-reviewer.md SOP file | Add \\\"Identifier Naming\\\" to standards checklist (exact path to be confirmed during implementation) |\\n\\nNote: Exact paths for the duplicate python3-standards.md and both code-reviewer.md SOP files were identified in the impact radius analysis; confirming paths is part of pre-implementation verification.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 all required information is available; no blocking items\\n- Blocks: Any backlog item that adds further Python naming guidance to python3-standards.md section 1.5 should be sequenced after this item to avoid conflicts\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the rule content is well-defined and sourced; the primary work is editing 2-5 existing markdown files, each requiring a bounded addition. The verbatim duplicate adds one extra file but is a copy operation. CI linting requirements (blank lines, language specifiers on fences) are known constraints. No research or design uncertainty remains.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: NOT_APPLICABLE\\n\\nRationale: This item adds a new rule to an empty section (1.5) in python3-standards.md. There is no existing implementation to diverge from \u2014 the section is unoccupied. The change is purely additive documentation. No design intent conflict is possible.\\n\\nScope: NARROW \u2014 alignment-analyst not spawned per scope sizing. Orchestrator assessed directly: additive rule to unoccupied section with no prior implementation.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale**: The system has no guardrail preventing agents from writing opaque acronym-based identifiers (gcd(), bfs(), dfs()). python3-standards.md covers type safety, architecture, error handling, performance, and testing but contains zero guidance on identifier naming. The fix is adding a validation gate (written rule with contrast examples) to prompt expansion of acronyms. No broken code to fix \u2014 the quality gate is absent.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Agents produce opaque acronym names (gcd(), bfs(), dfs()) without guidance \u2192 python3-standards.md rule prompts agents to expand acronyms in public APIs (greatest_common_divisor(), breadth_first_search(), depth_first_search())\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` covers type safety, architecture, error handling, performance, and testing. It contains zero guidance on identifier naming beyond mentioning that test names should be behavioral. There is no rule against single-letter or acronym function names.\\n\\nVerification: `grep -rn \\\"expand acron|gcd()|abbreviat\\\" plugins/python3-development/ plugins/python-engineering/` returns no matches in any SKILL.md or reference file.\\n\\nAs a result, agents writing Python code may produce identifiers like `gcd()`, `lcm()`, `bfs()`, `dfs()` without prompting expansion to `greatest_common_divisor()`, `breadth_first_search()`, etc., reducing readability of generated code for downstream consumers.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` covers type safety, architecture, error handling, performance, and testing. It contains zero guidance on identifier naming beyond mentioning that test names should be behavioral. There is no rule against single-letter or acronym function names.\\n\\nVerification: `grep -rn \\\"expand acron|gcd()|abbreviat\\\" plugins/python3-development/ plugins/python-engineering/` returns no matches in any SKILL.md or reference file.\\n\\nAs a result, agents writing Python code may produce identifiers like `gcd()`, `lcm()`, `bfs()`, `dfs()` without prompting expansion to `greatest_common_divisor()`, `breadth_first_search()`, etc., reducing readability of generated code for downstream consumers.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`python3-standards.md` Section 1.1 (or a new Section 1.X \\\"Identifier Naming\\\") includes an \\\"Expand Acronyms in Public APIs\\\" subsection with:\\n\\n1. The rule statement: \\\"Expand acronyms in function names, class names, and module names. `gcd()` is opaque; `greatest_common_divisor()` is self-documenting.\\\"\\n2. Two contrast examples \u2014 one acronym-named function and the same function with the acronym expanded\\n3. A scoped exception: established three-letter acronyms accepted as words (URL, API, SQL, HTTP, JSON, XML) may remain abbreviated when they are the standard term in the domain\\n4. A pointer to apply the same rule to local variables when the variable's lifetime exceeds 5 lines\\n\\nThe same content is added to `plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` Naming Defaults section, or `python3-core` references the python3-development standard if that file is the canonical source.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun: `grep -n \\\"expand acron|greatest_common_divisor\\\" plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` returns at least one match in a section heading or rule statement. The file contains the literal contrast example pair `gcd()` and `greatest_common_divisor()`. Cross-reference: `grep -n \\\"expand acron\\\" plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md` returns either the rule or an explicit pointer to the python3-development source.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Source Pattern\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFrom TheAlgorithms-Python.md \\\"Naming Conventions\\\" section, citing the project's CONTRIBUTING.md:\\n\\n> \\\"Expand acronyms because `gcd()` is hard to understand but `greatest_common_divisor()` is not.\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Confidence High\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nDirect grep across both python plugins confirms the rule is absent. The pattern from TheAlgorithms is concrete (single rule, exact contrast example provided). No interpretation required to identify the gap or the target text.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 1 Python3 Standards Md Coverage And Naming Guidance\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/python3-development/skills/python3-development/references/python3-standards.md (lines 1-211)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-04-27\\n- Content: |\\n    The document contains explicit sections for:\\n    - Type Safety & Modern Patterns (lines 7-20)\\n    - Architecture & Structure (lines 23-27)\\n    - Error Handling & Security (lines 29-36)\\n    - Performance (lines 38-42)\\n    - Testing guidance (lines 70-77)\\n    \\n    Naming conventions mentioned only at line 75: \\\"Test names must describe behavior, not implementation\\\"\\n    \\n    Zero guidance on identifier naming (acronyms, single letters, function names) beyond test naming.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: Full read of python3-standards.md 211 lines\\n- Finding: Document structure matches claim exactly. Five major domains specified. Naming guidance limited to test-name behavioral requirements only.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The document covers all five stated domains with zero guidance on identifier naming conventions beyond test names. The claim is factually accurate.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 2 Grep Search For Naming Acronym References Returns Zero Matches\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: Bash grep across /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/python3-development/ and /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/python-engineering/\\n- Command: grep -rn 'expand acron|gcd()|abbreviat' <paths>\\n- Retrieved: 2026-04-27\\n- Content: |\\n    (no output \u2014 zero matches returned)\\n    \\n    Verified across all SKILL.md files and reference files in both plugins.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: TheAlgorithms-Python.md line 150\\n- Finding: The ONLY occurrence of \\\"expand acronyms\\\" and \\\"gcd()\\\" pattern in the codebase is in TheAlgorithms-Python.md, NOT in any Python plugin skill or reference file.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Grep search correctly returns zero matches in the Python plugin skill documentation. The naming guidance exists only in the external learning-resources reference, not in plugin standards.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 3 Agents Writing Python Code Have No Prompting Against Acronyms\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: Logical consequence of Claims 1 and 2\\n- Retrieved: 2026-04-27\\n- Content: |\\n    With zero naming guidance in python3-standards.md (Claim 1) and zero grep matches for acronym expansion guidance in plugin skills (Claim 2), agents loading the Python standards have no instruction against acronym identifiers like gcd(), lcm(), bfs(), dfs().\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: python3-core/SKILL.md \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" section (lines 11-44)\\n- Finding: Standing Defaults cover code quality, type coverage, testing, tooling, and design principles. No identifier naming guidance present.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The plugin documentation provides no guidance prohibiting or discouraging acronym function names. Agents would default to whatever their base training suggests.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 4 Naming Defaults Section Existence In Python3 Core Skill Md\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md\\n- Retrieved: 2026-04-27\\n- Command: grep -n \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" /home/user/claude_skills/plugins/python-engineering/skills/python3-core/SKILL.md\\n- Content: |\\n    (no output \u2014 zero matches)\\n    \\n    Full read of SKILL.md reveals:\\n    - Section \\\"Standing Defaults (apply to every Python task)\\\" (lines 11-44)\\n    - Subsections: Code Quality, Type Coverage, Testing Defaults, Tooling, Design Principles\\n    - NO \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" section exists\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: Full read of python3-core/SKILL.md (168 lines)\\n- Finding: The file contains no section titled \\\"Naming Defaults\\\". The claim that such a section exists is false.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" section does not exist in python3-core/SKILL.md. The file contains only \\\"Standing Defaults\\\" grouped by Code Quality, Type Coverage, Testing, Tooling, and Design Principles.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 5 Thealgorithms Python Md Naming Conventions Section Attribution\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: /home/user/claude_skills/research/learning-resources/TheAlgorithms-Python.md (lines 142-150)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-04-27\\n- Content: |\\n    ### Naming Conventions\\n    \\n    - **Directories**: Plural, snake_case (e.g., `sorts`, `searches`, `data_structures`)\\n    - **Modules**: snake_case filenames matching the algorithm name (e.g., `bubble_sort.py` for bubble sort)\\n    - **Functions**: snake_case (e.g., `bubble_sort_iterative()`)\\n    - **Classes**: CamelCase (e.g., `LinkedListNode`)\\n    - **Constants**: UPPER_CASE (e.g., `MAX_ITERATIONS`)\\n    \\n    Per CONTRIBUTING.md: \\\"Expand acronyms because `gcd()` is hard to understand but `greatest_common_divisor()` is not.\\\"\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: TheAlgorithms/Python official repository CONTRIBUTING.md\\n- Finding: The quote matches the project's official guidance on naming conventions.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The exact citation is present in the document at line 150, verbatim. The claim is verified.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Claim | Verdict | Notes |\\n|-------|---------|-------|\\n| 1 | VERIFIED | python3-standards.md covers 5 domains; zero naming guidance beyond test names |\\n| 2 | VERIFIED | Grep search correctly returns zero matches in plugin skills |\\n| 3 | VERIFIED | Follows from Claims 1 & 2; no anti-acronym guidance in standards |\\n| 4 | REFUTED | \\\"Naming Defaults\\\" section does not exist in python3-core/SKILL.md |\\n| 5 | VERIFIED | Citation found exactly as quoted in TheAlgorithms-Python.md line 150 |\\n\\nVERIFIED: 4/5\\nREFUTED: 1/5\\nINCONCLUSIVE: 0/5\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ae62fe4ea5fa37e16658f68515673bb56804593c6fecdc919484e63db9740d1b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCbt3Q\"}"}